# /etc/ghostunnel/observers.env: what the operator expects, read by the
# four observer units and never by ghostunnel.service. Copy to
# /etc/ghostunnel/observers.env, mode 0644 root:root. It holds no secret.
#
# The second file is deliberate. ring.env is what ghostunnel is started
# with; an observer whose expectation is read from there compares the proxy's
# configuration with itself and can never disagree with it. Write this file
# from what the deployment is supposed to be (the change record, the
# inventory, the reviewed design), not by copying ring.env, and review the two
# separately. A deliberate reconfiguration is two edits; a change to ring.env
# alone halts the ring, which is the point (README, "Two files, two sources").
#
# Every variable must be set. The units refuse to start on an empty
# listener, target, ACL or binary checksum (ExecStartPre), because an empty
# ${VAR} in ExecStart would silently shift the next flag into its place; the
# two passed with '=' may be empty, and what empty means is stated at each.

# The listener ghostunnel is expected to bind: its --listen, verbatim. The
# tunnel observer halts if the start line names another.
GT_EXPECT_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8443

# The backend ghostunnel is expected to dial: its --target, verbatim,
# compared exactly (127.0.0.1:8080 and localhost:8080 are two targets). The
# tunnel observer halts if the start line names another.
GT_EXPECT_TARGET=127.0.0.1:8080

# What the backend is expected to be handed ahead of each connection's
# bytes: the start line's proxy_protocol, one of
#   off       nothing (no --proxy-protocol, no --proxy-protocol-mode)
#   conn      a PROXY protocol v2 header with the connection's addresses
#   tls       that header with the TLS version, ALPN and SNI
#   tls-full  all of that and the client's certificate, whole
# Empty means off. There is no way to expect nothing: the tunnel observer
# halts if the start line's mode is not this one, so a backend handed a
# header, or a certificate, nobody wrote down here is a halt.
GT_EXPECT_PROXY_PROTOCOL=

# The access-control rules ghostunnel is expected to run with, as the start
# line carries them: a comma-separated list, no spaces, one entry per rule,
# from the vocabulary
#   allow-all  allow-cn:<v>  allow-ou:<v>  allow-dns:<v>  allow-uri:<v>
#   allow-ip:<v>  allow-spki-pin:<hex>  policy:<sha256>
# (policy:<sha256> is the start line's entry for --allow-policy). The tunnel
# observer halts if the start line's set differs. For ring.env's example
# GT_ACL=--allow-cn client.example.com the expectation is:
GT_EXPECT_ACL=allow-cn:client.example.com

# The SHA-256 of the ghostunnel executable the proxy is expected to run, 64
# lower-case hex characters. The material observer halts unless the start
# line records exactly this hash and the file at the recorded path still
# hashes to it. Take it where the build is produced and reviewed, before
# the binary is copied to this host: sha256sum of the build output there,
# or the SHA-256 in the SPDX document go tool mage sbom:generate writes
# beside that output (not beside /usr/local/bin/ghostunnel):
#   go tool mage sbom:generate out/ghostunnel
#   jq -r '.files[0].checksums[] | select(.algorithm=="SHA256") | .checksumValue' out/ghostunnel.spdx.json
# Upstream's release SBOMs cover upstream's binaries only; those carry no
# ring and are not this proxy. A value copied from the host holds the ring
# to whatever the host runs.
GT_EXPECT_BINARY_SHA256=

# The OPA query ghostunnel is expected to evaluate its --allow-policy with
# (its --allow-query, verbatim), for acl-substance: every member re-runs the
# policy the start line names by hash on each served handshake's certificate
# with this query and compares the outcome with what ghostunnel recorded. The
# trace does not carry the query, and it must not: a proxy could record a
# query that always allows beside the hashed policy file. It is read by all
# four observer units, as one value; it may be empty when no policy rule is
# deployed, and must be set when one is, or acl-substance fails on every
# access decision under the policy (a check that cannot run has not passed).
GT_EXPECT_POLICY_QUERY=
